#prob(title: [Curtis 2.45])[
    Relative to an earth-centered, nonrotating frame, the position and velocity vectors of a spacecraft are $vbu(r)_0 = 6320 vuu(i) + 7750 vuu(k)$ (#unit("km")) and $vbu(v)_0 = 11 vuu(j)$ (#unit("km/s")).
    Use the $Delta theta$ Lagrange coefficient method.

    + Find the position vector #qty(10, "min") later.
    <hwk:p01a>

    + Calculate the change in true anomaly over the #qty(10, "min") time span.
    <hwk:p01b>
] <hwk:p01>

+ With $mu = qty(398600, "km^3/s^2")$, the initial radius and radial velocity are:
    $
        r_0 & = sqrt(6320^2 + 7750^2) space unit("km") = qty(result("01a", "r0"), "km"), \
        v_(r_0) & = (vbu(r)_0 dot vbu(v)_0)/r_0 = 0.
    $

    The angular momentum, eccentricity vector, and specific energy are:
    $
        vbu(h) & = vbu(r)_0 times vbu(v)_0 = vecrow(-85250, 0, 69520) space (unit("km^2/s")), quad h = qty(result("01a", "h"), "km^2/s"), \
        vbu(e) & = (vbu(v)_0 times vbu(h))/mu - vbu(r)_0/r_0 = vecrow(#result("01a", "e_x"), 0, #result("01a", "e_z")), quad e = #result("01a", "e"), \
        cal(E) & = v_0^2/2 - mu/r_0 = qty(result("01a", "energy"), "km^2/s^2").
    $

    Since $e > 1$ and $cal(E) > 0$, the trajectory is a hyperbola. Furthermore, $v_(r_0) = 0$ and $vbu(e)$ is parallel to $vbu(r)_0$, so the spacecraft is at perigee at $t = 0$, meaning $theta_0 = 0$.

    The $Delta theta$ Lagrange coefficients require the true anomaly swept in the #qty(10, "min") coast, which we obtain from Kepler's equation for the hyperbola. With $t = qty(600, "s")$ measured from perigee, the hyperbolic mean anomaly is:
    $
        M_h = mu^2/h^3 (e^2 - 1)^(3/2) t = #result("01a", "M_h").
    $

    Solving $e sinh(F) - F = M_h$ for the hyperbolic eccentric anomaly $F$ via Newton's method, starting at $F_0 = M_h$:
    $
        F_(k+1) = F_k - (e sinh(F_k) - F_k - M_h)/(e cosh(F_k) - 1),
    $
    converges to $F = #result("01a", "F")$. Converting to true anomaly,
    $
                         tan(theta/2) & = sqrt((e + 1)/(e - 1)) tanh(F/2), \
                                theta & = qty(result("01a", "theta"), "degree"), \
        Delta theta = theta - theta_0 & = qty(result("01a", "dtheta"), "degree").
    $ <eqn:s01-dtheta>

    The orbit equation in terms of $Delta theta$ then gives the radius at $t = qty(10, "min")$:
    $
        r = h^2/mu 1/(1 + (h^2/(mu r_0) - 1) cos(Delta theta) - (h v_(r_0))/mu sin(Delta theta)) = qty(result("01a", "r"), "km").
    $

    The Lagrange coefficients are:
    $
        f & = 1 - (mu r)/h^2 (1 - cos(Delta theta)) = #result("01a", "f"), \
        g & = (r r_0)/h sin(Delta theta) = qty(result("01a", "g"), "s"), \
        dot(f) & = mu/h (1 - cos(Delta theta))/sin(Delta theta) [mu/h^2 (1 - cos(Delta theta)) - 1/r_0 - 1/r] = qty(result("01a", "f_dot"), "/s"), \
        dot(g) & = 1 - (mu r_0)/h^2 (1 - cos(Delta theta)) = #result("01a", "g_dot").
    $

    Thus, the position vector #qty(10, "min") later is:
    $
        vbu(r) & = f vbu(r)_0 + g vbu(v)_0 \
        & = #result("01a", "r_x") vuu(i) + #result("01a", "r_y") vuu(j) + #result("01a", "r_z") vuu(k) space (unit("km")) quad qed
    $

    As a check, $abs(vbu(r)) = qty(result("01a", "r"), "km")$ matches the orbit equation, $f dot(g) - dot(f) g = #result("01a", "lagrange identity")$, and $vbu(v) = dot(f) vbu(r)_0 + dot(g) vbu(v)_0 = vecrow(#result("01a", "v_x"), #result("01a", "v_y"), #result("01a", "v_z")) space (unit("km/s"))$ conserves both $vbu(h)$ and $cal(E)$. A direct RK4 integration of $dot.double(vbu(r)) = -mu vbu(r) \/ r^3$ over the same #qty(600, "s") also reproduces $vbu(r)$ to within #qty(1, "mm").

    #figure(
        image("../outputs/figures/s01a.png", width: 55%),
        caption: [Hyperbolic trajectory in the perifocal frame, with the #qty(10, "min") coast from perigee highlighted.],
    ) <fig:s01a>
<hwk:s01a>

+ From #link(<eqn:s01-dtheta>)[part (a)], the spacecraft starts at perigee ($theta_0 = 0$) and reaches $theta = qty(result("01b", "theta"), "degree")$ after #qty(10, "min"), so the change in true anomaly is:
    $
        Delta theta = theta - theta_0 = qty(result("01b", "dtheta"), "degree") quad qed
    $

    As an independent check that bypasses Kepler's equation and the Lagrange coefficients entirely, numerically integrating $dot.double(vbu(r)) = -mu vbu(r) \/ r^3$ from $(vbu(r)_0, vbu(v)_0)$ for #qty(600, "s") with RK4 gives $vbu(r)_"RK4" = vecrow(#result("01b", "r_rk4_x"), #result("01b", "r_rk4_y"), #result("01b", "r_rk4_z")) space (unit("km"))$. The angle between the two position vectors is then:
    $
        cos(Delta theta) = (vbu(r)_0 dot vbu(r)_"RK4")/(r_0 abs(vbu(r)_"RK4")) = #result("01b", "cos dtheta rk4"),
    $
    and $(vbu(r)_0 times vbu(r)_"RK4") dot vbu(h) > 0$, so the spacecraft swept $Delta theta = qty(result("01b", "swept rk4"), "degree")$ in the direction of motion, in agreement with the above.
<hwk:s01b>

