#prob(title: [Curtis 7.10])[
    At time $t = 0$, a particle is at the origin of a CW frame with a relative velocity $delta vbu(v)_0 = v vuu(j)$. What will be the relative speed of the particle after a time equal to one-half the orbital period of the CW frame?
] <hwk:p04>

The particle starts at the origin, so $delta vbu(r)_0 = vb(0)$ and only the $Phi_(v v)$ term of #link(<eqn:cw>)[the CW solution] survives in the relative velocity:
$
    delta vbu(v) (t) = Phi_(v v) (t) delta vbu(v)_0 = mat(
        cos(n t), 2 sin(n t), 0;
        -2 sin(n t), 4 cos(n t) - 3, 0;
        0, 0, cos(n t)
    ) vec(0, v, 0) = v vec(2 sin(n t), 4 cos(n t) - 3, 0).
$

One-half of the orbital period is $t = T/2 = pi/n$, so $n t = pi$, where $sin(pi) = 0$ and $cos(pi) = -1$:
$
    delta vbu(v) (T/2) = v vec(2 sin(pi), 4 cos(pi) - 3, 0) = vec(0, -7 v, 0).
$

Thus, the relative speed after one-half of the orbital period is:
$
    abs(delta vbu(v) (T/2)) = 7 abs(v) quad qed
$
i.e., seven times the initial relative speed, now directed along $-vuu(j)$.

More generally, the relative speed is:
$
    abs(delta vbu(v)) = abs(v) sqrt(4 sin^2(n t) + (4 cos(n t) - 3)^2) = abs(v) sqrt(13 - 24 cos(n t) + 12 cos^2(n t)),
$
whose radicand has derivative $24 sin(n t) (1 - cos(n t)) >= 0$ on $0 <= n t <= pi$. The speed therefore grows monotonically from $abs(v)$ to its maximum of $7 abs(v)$ at the half period. At that instant, $delta vbu(r) = Phi_(r v) delta vbu(v)_0 = (v\/n) vecrow(4, -3 pi, 0)$.

#figure(
    image("../outputs/figures/s04.png", width: 90%),
    caption: [Nondimensional relative trajectory (left) and relative speed (right) over one-half of the orbital period.],
)
<hwk:s04>

