#prob(title: [Orbit Propagation with STM])[
    + Using Lagrange Coefficients, find the position, velocity, and time for this orbit #qty(30, "min") after epoch:
        $
            vb(r)_1 = mat(5740.13268349; 3314.06715; 0) quad (unit("km")) \
            dot(vb(r))_1 = mat(-2.75082684; 4.76457184; 5.5016536) quad (unit("km/s"))
        $
    <hwk:p05a>

    + Find the two-body state transition matrix over #qty(30, "min") using Battin's technique for computing the two-body state transition matrix as described by #link("https://doi.org/10.2514/1.G001030")[Arora et. al, Section IV.A] and in class.
    <hwk:p05b>

    +
        #link("https://tellurion.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html")[Tellurion] will #link("https://tellurion.readthedocs.io/en/latest/prop.html")[propagate] and #link("https://tellurion.readthedocs.io/en/latest/prop.html#state-transition-matrix-stm")[compute the state transition matrix] simultaneously. You should specify the #link("https://tellurion.readthedocs.io/en/latest/prop.html#keplerian-two-body")[two-body propagator], and refer to the #link("https://tellurion.readthedocs.io/en/latest/tutorials/stm1.html")[STM tutorial] for examples on how to specify it. Although Tellurion requires an epoch time, what you choose for the initial state will not matter because of the two-body propagation; if you use #raw("tell.abstime(0.0)") it will use the current time.

        Propagate using the two-body propagator in Tellurion and compute the state transition matrix. Compare with your results above and explain any differences you find.
    <hwk:p05c>
] <hwk:p05>

+ As in class, write the Lagrange coefficients in terms of the change in eccentric anomaly $Delta E$. With $mu = qty(398600, "km^3/s^2")$, the initial state gives:
    $
        r_1 & = abs(vbu(r)_1) = qty(result("05a", "r1"), "km"), quad v_1 = abs(dot(vbu(r))_1) = qty(result("05a", "v1"), "km/s"), \
        a & = (2/r_1 - v_1^2/mu)^(-1) = qty(result("05a", "a"), "km"), \
        sigma_1 & = (vbu(r)_1 dot dot(vbu(r))_1)/sqrt(mu) = #result("05a", "sigma1") space sqrt(unit("km")).
    $

    The orbit is a nearly circular ellipse ($e = #result("05a", "e")$, $T = qty(result("05a", "period"), "s")$), so #qty(30, "min") is about one-third of a revolution.

    Since $r = a (1 - e cos E)$ and $vbu(r) dot dot(vbu(r)) = sqrt(mu a) e sin E$, the initial eccentric anomaly enters only through
    $
        e cos(E_1) = 1 - r_1/a, quad e sin(E_1) = sigma_1/sqrt(a).
    $

    Expanding $sin(E_1 + Delta E)$ in Kepler's equation, $n Delta t = Delta E - e (sin(E_2) - sin(E_1))$, gives the equation for $Delta E$:
    $
        n Delta t = Delta E - (1 - r_1/a) sin(Delta E) + sigma_1/sqrt(a) (1 - cos(Delta E)), quad n = sqrt(mu/a^3).
    $ <eqn:s05-kepler>

    With $Delta t = qty(1800, "s")$, $n Delta t = qty(result("05a", "M"), "rad")$. Newton's method (derivative $r\/a > 0$) converges to:
    $
        Delta E = qty(result("05a", "dE"), "rad") = qty(result("05a", "dE_deg"), "degree").
    $

    The radius at $t_2$ and the Lagrange coefficients are then:
    $
        r_2 & = a + (r_1 - a) cos(Delta E) + sigma_1 sqrt(a) sin(Delta E) = qty(result("05a", "r2"), "km"), \
        f & = 1 - a/r_1 (1 - cos(Delta E)) = #result("05a", "f"), \
        g & = Delta t - sqrt(a^3/mu) (Delta E - sin(Delta E)) = qty(result("05a", "g"), "s"), \
        dot(f) & = (-sqrt(mu a) sin(Delta E))/(r_2 r_1) = qty(result("05a", "f_dot"), "/s"), \
        dot(g) & = 1 - a/r_2 (1 - cos(Delta E)) = #result("05a", "g_dot").
    $ <eqn:s05-lagrange>

    Thus, #qty(30, "min") after epoch, the position, velocity, and time are:
    $
        vbu(r)_2 & = f vbu(r)_1 + g dot(vbu(r))_1 = vecrow(#result("05a", "r2_x"), #result("05a", "r2_y"), #result("05a", "r2_z")) space (unit("km")), \
        dot(vbu(r))_2 & = dot(f) vbu(r)_1 + dot(g) dot(vbu(r))_1 = vecrow(#result("05a", "v2_x"), #result("05a", "v2_y"), #result("05a", "v2_z")) space (unit("km/s")), \
        t_2 & = t_1 + qty(1800, "s") quad qed
    $

    With the epoch used in #link(<hwk:s05c>)[part (c)], $t_1 =$ 2026-10-21 00:00:00 UTC, this is $t_2 =$ 2026-10-21 00:30:00 UTC. As a check, $f dot(g) - dot(f) g = #result("05a", "lagrange identity")$, $vbu(h)$ and $cal(E)$ are conserved, and an RK4 integration of $dot.double(vbu(r)) = -mu vbu(r) \/ r^3$ reproduces $vbu(r)_2$ to within #qty(1, "mm").
<hwk:s05a>

+ Battin's state transition matrix (Arora et al., Section IV.A) gives the Jacobian of the final state with respect to the initial state in four $3 times 3$ blocks:
    $
        Phi(t_2, t_1) = mat(A, B; C, D) = mat(pdv(vbu(r)_2, vbu(r)_1), pdv(vbu(r)_2, dot(vbu(r))_1); pdv(dot(vbu(r))_2, vbu(r)_1), pdv(dot(vbu(r))_2, dot(vbu(r))_1)).
    $

    The blocks use the universal functions $U_1$--$U_5$, which follow from the Lagrange coefficients of #link(<eqn:s05-lagrange>)[part (a)] (Eqs. 38--44, 50):
    $
        alpha & = 2/r_1 - v_1^2/mu, quad sigma_i = (vbu(r)_i dot dot(vbu(r))_i)/sqrt(mu), quad chi = alpha sqrt(mu) Delta t + sigma_2 - sigma_1, \
        U_1 & = -(r_2 r_1 dot(f))/sqrt(mu), quad U_2 = r_1 (1 - f), quad U_3 = sqrt(mu) (Delta t - g), \
        U_4 & = U_1 U_3 - 1/2 (U_2^2 - alpha U_3^2), quad U_5 = (chi^3\/6 - U_3)/alpha, \
        overline(C) & = 1/sqrt(mu) (3 U_5 - chi U_4 - sqrt(mu) Delta t U_2).
    $

    Substituting:
    $
        alpha & = qty(result("05b", "alpha"), "/km"), quad sigma_2 = num(result("05b", "sigma2")) space sqrt(unit("km")), quad chi = num(result("05b", "chi")) space sqrt(unit("km")), \
        U_1 & = num(result("05b", "U1")) space sqrt(unit("km")), quad U_2 = qty(result("05b", "U2"), "km"), quad U_3 = num(result("05b", "U3")) space upright("km")^(3\/2), \
        U_4 & = qty(result("05b", "U4"), "km^2"), quad U_5 = num(result("05b", "U5")) space upright("km")^(5\/2), \
        overline(C) & = qty(result("05b", "C_bar"), "km s").
