+ Following the method from class (Prussing and Conway), Lambert's theorem makes the flight time a function of only the semimajor axis $a$, the radius sum $r_1 + r_2$, the chord $c$, and the direction (short or long way). With $mu = qty(398600, "km^3/s^2")$:
    $
        r_1 & = qty(result("06a", "r1"), "km"), quad r_2 = qty(result("06a", "r2"), "km"), \
        c & = abs(vbu(r)_2 - vbu(r)_1) = qty(result("06a", "c"), "km"), quad s = (r_1 + r_2 + c)/2 = qty(result("06a", "s"), "km"), \
        cos(Delta theta) & = (vbu(r)_1 dot vbu(r)_2)/(r_1 r_2) = #result("06a", "cos dtheta").
    $

    The zero-revolution problem has two solutions. The short way has $Delta theta = qty(result("06a", "dtheta_short"), "degree") < qty(180, "degree")$. Its angular momentum points along $vbu(r)_1 times vbu(r)_2$, and since $(vbu(r)_1 times vbu(r)_2) dot vuu(K) < 0$, the short way is retrograde ($i > qty(90, "degree")$). The long way has $Delta theta = qty(result("06a", "dtheta_long"), "degree")$ and travels in the opposite, prograde sense. Because $Delta theta$ is close to #qty(180, "degree"), the two transfers need nearly the same semimajor axis.

    For a trial $a$, the angles $alpha$ and $beta$ follow from
    $
        sin^2(alpha^(*)/2) = s/(2a), quad sin^2(beta^(*)/2) = (s - c)/(2a), quad 0 <= alpha^(*), beta^(*) <= pi,
    $
    with $beta = beta^(*)$ on the short way and $beta = -beta^(*)$ on the long way. The flight time is Lagrange's equation:
    $
        Delta t(a) = 1/n [alpha - beta - (sin(alpha) - sin(beta))], quad n = sqrt(mu/a^3).
    $ <eqn:s06-lagrange-time>

    Two limits fix the branch:
    - The minimum-energy ellipse, $a_min = s\/2 = qty(result("06a", "a_min"), "km")$ with $alpha = pi$, has flight time $t_c = [pi - (beta_c - sin(beta_c))] \/ n_c$, where $sin^2(beta_c\/2) = (s - c)\/s$. If $Delta t < t_c$ then $alpha = alpha^(*)$; otherwise $alpha = 2 pi - alpha^(*)$.
    - The parabolic (Euler) time $t_p = [(r_1 + r_2 + c)^(3\/2) minus.plus (r_1 + r_2 - c)^(3\/2)] \/ (6 sqrt(mu))$ bounds the ellipses, with the upper sign for the short way and the lower sign for the long way: $Delta t > t_p$ is required.

    Starting from $a_min$, bracketing and bisecting on $a$ until #link(<eqn:s06-lagrange-time>)[the flight-time equation] matches $Delta t = qty(5200, "s")$ gives $Delta E = alpha - beta$. The class $Delta E$ Lagrange coefficients then give the velocities:
    $
        f = 1 - a/r_1 (1 - cos(Delta E)), quad g = Delta t - sqrt(a^3/mu) (Delta E - sin(Delta E)), quad dot(g) = 1 - a/r_2 (1 - cos(Delta E)), \
        dot(vbu(r))_1 = (vbu(r)_2 - f vbu(r)_1)/g, quad dot(vbu(r))_2 = (dot(g) vbu(r)_2 - vbu(r)_1)/g.
    $

    #let lambertRows = (
        ($t_p$, "t_p", "s"),
        ($beta_c$, "beta_c", "degree"),
        ($t_c$, "t_c", "s"),
        ($a$, "a", "km"),
